# The seven days, their order, and today and tomorrow, on a weather chart for the ferry town's week.
lesson language-days v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=8 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "[[time.monday|cap]], [[time.tuesday]]"
  goal "Say the days of the week in [[language.name]] in order, and say what day it is today and tomorrow."

  look {
    scene 31x9 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        weatherweek week days=["[[time.monday]]", "[[time.tuesday]]", "[[time.wednesday]]", "[[time.thursday]]", "[[time.friday]]", "[[time.saturday]]", "[[time.sunday]]"] skies=["sun", "sun", "cloud", "rain", "sunny-spells", "sun", "sun"] temps=["", "", "", "", "", "", ""]
      }
    }
    say """
      The week: [[time.monday]], [[time.tuesday]], [[time.wednesday]], [[time.thursday]],
      [[time.friday]], [[time.saturday]], [[time.sunday]]. Saturday and Sunday are the weekend.
      Today is [[time.today]] and tomorrow is [[time.tomorrow]]: [[time.today-is-monday]]
      [[phrase.tomorrow-is-tuesday]]
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.days-name k=3
    }
    show language.days-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show language.days-meaning k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.days-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.days-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.days-match k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=3
    show language.days-write k=5 easy-k=4 hard-k=3
    show language.days-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.days-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.days-write k=2
    }
  }
  language es {
    try stars=2 {
      show language.es-days-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.days-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[time.monday|cap]], [[time.tuesday]], [[time.wednesday]], [[time.thursday]], [[time.friday]], [[time.saturday]], [[time.sunday]]. [[time.today|cap]] is today, [[time.tomorrow]] is tomorrow."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two neighbouring days swapped. The ringed card in the naming
    questions shows the English day, so on paper the question still reads. The listening question is
    a dictation: read the sentence aloud from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, slowly and
    twice, or let the device play it. It is marked word by word, and a missing accent counts. If the
    child is stuck, sing the days to a tune you know, pointing at each card. Off the page, say what
    day it is in the language at breakfast each morning this week. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "listen attentively to spoken language
    and show understanding by joining in and responding"; Japan's grades 3 and 4 foreign language
    activities: "Activities to talk about their own thoughts and feelings regarding familiar and
    simple matters in daily life such as times, days of the week and places"; and CEFR A1, "Can
    understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic phrases". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[time.tuesday]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[time.monday]] and [[time.sunday]] and the words to write are [[time.friday]] and [[time.saturday]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[time.saturday]] and [[time.thursday]] and the words to write are [[time.wednesday]], [[time.tuesday]] and [[time.friday]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
